          "depth": "core",
          "heading": "Closest to home: the Gemeinden",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The third and lowest tier is the **Gemeinde** (municipality) — your town or city. Germany's administration runs in three steps: **Bund → Länder → Gemeinden**, and the Gemeinde is the bottom rung, the level nearest the citizen. When the test asks for the \"lowest political level,\" the answer is *Gemeinden* — not city councils or district administrators, which are bodies *within* that level rather than the level itself."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The head of a municipality is the **Bürgermeister/in** (mayor) — literally the *Oberhaupt einer Gemeinde*, the head of the local community. Not the head of a school, not the boss of a bank, not the chair of a party: the mayor leads the local administration and, depending on the state, is elected either directly by residents or by the municipal council."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Local government also runs its own offices, and the name of an office usually reveals which tier it sits on. The **Ordnungsamt** (public-order office) — handling things like local permits, registration and public order — is part of the *Gemeindeverwaltung* (municipal administration). Contrast that with the **Finanzamt** (tax office), which is a *Land* authority, and the **Auswärtiges Amt** (Foreign Office), which is a *federal* ministry. A *Pfarramt*, by the way, is a church parish office and not part of the state at all."
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "tone": "key",
              "title": "Federalism reaches your town hall",
              "text": "Municipal self-government is not a favour granted from above — it is guaranteed by **Article 28** of the Grundgesetz, which gives every Gemeinde the right to regulate \"all the affairs of the local community\" on its own responsibility within the law."
            }
          ]
