              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The process started with the raw materials of war. The 1951 **Treaty of Paris** placed the coal and steel of six states — France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg — under joint control (the Coal and Steel Community). Its driving motive was Franco-German reconciliation: if you can't build weapons in secret, you can't start a war in secret. Six years later came the decisive step. The **Römische Verträge** (*Treaties of Rome*), signed in 1957 and in force from 1958, founded the **Europäische Wirtschaftsgemeinschaft (EWG)** — the European Economic Community (EEC) — a common market among the same six states: West Germany, France, Italy, and the three Benelux countries (Belgium, the Netherlands, Luxembourg)."
