        {
          "depth": "deeper",
          "heading": "EU, NATO, UN, Warsaw Pact — don't mix them up",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The test deliberately lists several international organisations side by side, because Germany's relationship to each one is different — and only one of them is a case of *founding* membership."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "**EU (Europäische Union):** Germany is a *founding member* — from the 1950s communities onward. This is the one the test means when it asks what Germany co-founded.",
                "**NATO (Nordatlantikpakt):** the western military alliance. NATO was founded in 1949; West Germany only *joined* in 1955 — a member, but not a founder.",
                "**UN (Vereinte Nationen):** founded in 1945. Both West and East Germany joined only in 1973 — again members, not founders.",
                "**Warschauer Pakt (Warsaw Pact):** the Soviet-led military alliance of the Eastern bloc. West Germany was *never* a member; East Germany (the DDR) was. It dissolved in 1991."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reunification connects to this directly. When the **DDR** joined the Federal Republic in 1990, the five new eastern states automatically became part of everything West Germany already belonged to — including the European Community, today's EU. So German reunification also expanded the EU eastward, with no separate accession procedure for the east. The eastern states did *not* join the Warsaw Pact (it was collapsing) or any body Germany was not already in."
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "tone": "key",
              "title": "Memory hook",
              "text": "Founding member = the EU, and only the EU. NATO → joined 1955. UN → joined 1973. Warsaw Pact → never (that was the DDR). If a question asks what Germany *co-founded*, the answer is the EU / its predecessor communities."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "depth": "deeper",
          "heading": "Why the European Parliament has three cities",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "One detail confuses many test-takers: the European Parliament is not in a single place. Its official seat (*Sitz*) is **Straßburg** (*Strasbourg*), a French city right on the German border. The choice is symbolic — the surrounding region of Alsace was fought over and changed hands between France and Germany again and again, so putting the Parliament there represents Franco-German reconciliation. The formal plenary sessions are held in Strasbourg."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "But the Parliament actually works across **three** locations: **Straßburg** (official seat and plenary sessions), **Brüssel** (*Brussels*, where the committees meet and additional sessions are held), and **Luxemburg** (*Luxembourg*, home to the administrative offices — the General Secretariat). So the answer to \"where does the Parliament work?\" is Strasbourg, Luxembourg and Brussels together."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The distractors are worth knowing so they don't catch you: **London** never hosted the Parliament, and the UK has left the EU anyway; **Paris** is the French capital but not a Parliament seat; **Berlin** is the seat of the German **Bundestag**, not the European Parliament; and **Bern** and **Zürich** are in Switzerland, which is not even an EU member."
            },
            {
              "type": "note",
              "tone": "info",
              "title": "Read the question carefully",
              "text": "If it asks for *one* seat of the Parliament, the answer is Strasbourg. If it asks for the places where it *works*, the answer is the trio Strasbourg, Luxembourg and Brussels."
            }
          ]
        }
